    "reply": "The retrieved records cover EGFR-directed clinical studies in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and a set of ClinVar EGFR sequence variants on transcript NM_005228.5. They name the trials but give no recruitment status, and the variant records carry no clinical-significance or NSCLC annotation [1].\n\n## EGFR-TKI Clinical Studies\n\nSeveral studies examine EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy in NSCLC. One is A Retrospective Study of EGFR-TKIs,Gefitinib, Erlotinib and Osimertinib in NSCLC Patients Treatment [1]. Another is Survival Outcome of EGFR-TKI in Uncommon EGFR Mutant Advanced NSCLC [2]. A third is Osimertinib for Advanced EGFR-positive NSCLC Patients [3].\n\n## Other Trial Directions\n\nBeyond TKIs, one study addresses central nervous system spread: CSF Analysis in EGFR Mutant Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer With Leptomeningeal Disease [4]. A cellular-therapy study is EGFR/B7H3 CAR-T on Lung Cancer and Triple Negative Breast Cancer [5]. None of these records states whether the study is currently recruiting, so this should be confirmed on each ClinicalTrials.gov record [5].\n\n## ClinVar EGFR Variants\n\nThe graph holds many missense variants spread across EGFR. One example is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.840C>A (p.Asn280Lys), recorded as ClinVar:1000076 [6]. Another is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.2339T>C (p.Ile780Thr) [17]. A further example is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.2619A>T (p.Gly873=), which is a synonymous change [16]. These records do not link the variants to NSCLC or to response to EGFR-TKI therapy [30].",
